### Vulnerability Analysis
The `_.template` function in `lodash` is vulnerable to arbitrary code execution (Command Injection) via the `options.imports` option (CVE-2021-44524).

When compiling a template, `_.template` extracts the keys of `options.imports` using `keys(imports)` (line 14851) and passes them as parameter names to the `Function` constructor (line 14942). In ECMAScript 6, parameter definitions can contain default values (e.g., `param = defaultValue`). The `Function` constructor compiles the string of parameter names directly, allowing the default value expression to be parsed as executable code.

Because the compiled function is immediately executed using `.apply(undefined, importsValues)` (line 14943) and the values corresponding to these keys in `importsValues` can be set to `undefined`, JavaScript falls back to evaluating the default parameter expression. Since there is no validation or sanitization on the keys of `options.imports`, an attacker who can control or pollute the keys of the `options.imports` object can inject and execute arbitrary JavaScript code.

### Conceptual Exploit Payload
If an application exposes `options` of `_.template` to untrusted user input, or merges user input into template options:

```javascript
const _ = require('lodash');

// Injecting arbitrary code through the key of imports with an undefined value
const maliciousOptions = {
  imports: {
    'payload = console.log(process.mainModule.require("child_process").execSync("id").toString())': undefined
  }
};

// This immediately triggers the evaluation of the default parameter, executing the command 'id'
_.template('hello', maliciousOptions);
```

